        "entity_id": "christian_de_duve",
        "role": "subject"
      },
      {
        "entity_id": "alex_b_novikoff",
        "role": "collaborator"
      }
    ],
    "sources": [
      {
        "source_id": "opperdoes_2013",
        "page": 2,
        "quote": "Later, together with Alex B. Novikoff of the Albert Einstein College of Medicine in New York, de Duve first saw lysosomes when he made electron micrographs of highly purified cell fractions."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "peroxisome_discovery",
    "event_type": "invention",
    "label": "Discovered peroxisome",
    "date": {
      "display": "A few years later",
      "precision": "circa",
      "sort_start": "1958-01-01",
      "sort_end": "1962-12-31"
    },
    "participants": [
      {
        "entity_id": "christian_de_duve",
        "role": "subject"
      },
      {
        "entity_id": "peroxisome",
        "role": "invention"
      }
    ],
    "sources": [
      {
        "source_id": "opperdoes_2013",
        "page": 2,
        "quote": "A few years later he discovered a second organelle, which was characterized by the marker enzyme catalase and was loaded mostly with enzymes involved in a dangerous form of oxidation, releasing toxic peroxides, explaining why such reactions should be enclosed within a separate membrane-bound compartment. For this organelle de Duve coined the name peroxisome."
      }
    ]
  },
